### Identify the picker when its flyout button is missing or ambiguous

**Severity:** `low`

In `SetPicker`, `.Single(...)` throws a generic sequence exception if the inspected tree contains zero or multiple `FlyoutButton` matches. Keep the exactly-one requirement, but include `cardId` and the inspected tree in the assertion. If left unchanged, failures are harder to diagnose; this is a test-diagnostic improvement, not a demonstrated Awake product failure.

```suggestion
        var buttons = Nodes(tree).Where(node => node.TryGetProperty("automationId", out var id) && id.GetString() == "FlyoutButton").ToArray();
        Assert.AreEqual(1, buttons.Length, $"{cardId} must expose exactly one picker flyout button. Tree: {tree}");
        var button = buttons[0];
```

The affected test project compiles with this minimal replacement. Synthetic zero/one/multiple-match controls verify the diagnostic and preserved unique-button selection; they do not exercise the real picker UI.
